# Type Definition Rules

## All Interfaces Live in `src/types/index.ts`
Never define props interfaces inside component files. All public-facing types are exported from `src/types/index.ts` and re-exported via `src/index.ts` with `export * from "./types"`.

## Interface Naming
- Table component props: `TableProps<T>`
- Sub-component props: `ActionDropdownProps<T>`, `PaginationComponentProps`, `TableSkeletonProps`, `NoContentProps`
- Internal helpers: prefix with `I` or suffix with `Config` (e.g., `CellExpansionConfig`)

## Generic Constraints
```ts
// Always use T for row data — never object or any
interface TableProps<T> {
  data: T[];
  props: ReadonlyArray<keyof T>;   // keyof T enforces type-safe column access
  sortableProps?: Array<keyof T>;
  formatValue?: (value: string, prop: string, item: T) => React.ReactNode;
}
```

## Optional Props with Nested Objects
Group related optional config into named nested objects:
```ts
cellExpansion?: {
  enabled?: boolean;
  maxWidth?: string | number;
  behavior?: "truncate" | "wrap" | "expand";
};
accessibility?: {
  keyboardNavigation?: boolean;
  screenReaderLabels?: { actions?: string; pagination?: string; loading?: string };
};
```

## customClassNames Shape
The `customClassNames` prop mirrors the DOM structure of the rendered table.
Every element the consumer might want to style has its own key.
New styled elements added to components MUST get a corresponding key in `TableProps["customClassNames"]`.

## No `any`
Biome enforces `noExplicitAny: "error"`. Use:
- `unknown` for truly unknown values, then narrow with type guards
- `React.ReactNode` for renderable content
- `Record<string, boolean>` for string-keyed boolean maps
